id: regexp/syntax/s5-named-references/0012
area: regexp
op: RegExpInitialize
in: str:"[\\k<a>](?<a>x)" str:""
out: value: bool:true
class: impl-defined
per-engine.broiler-js: value: bool:true
per-engine.broiler-vm: value: bool:true
divergence.broiler-js: row S5 as revised 2026-11-01 (owner's decision D-s5): \k in a class beside a named group without u or v, [\k<a>](?<a>x) and [\k](?<a>x), by the constructor, a literal never evaluated and an exec; Broiler.JS answers refused, a SyntaxError "invalid named reference": RegExpGroupNames refuses a \k inside a class whenever the pattern holds a group name, where B.1.2's ClassEscape reads ~NamedCaptureGroups and admits k as an identity escape, the outcome this vector's per-engine field holds (regexpref's reading, which revision 31 took); a correction of Broiler.JS (JSD-0081, by D-s5); answered by SJL-11.5, which deletes RegExpGroupNames' regular-expression prechecks (decision D20)
prov: spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-regexpinitialize spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-parsepattern spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-patterns spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-parsepattern-annexb spec:es2026@0248456c#sec-regular-expressions-patterns ref:regexpref@1
script: new RegExp("[\\k<a>](?<a>x)", "") instanceof RegExp
note: correspondence row S5: AtomEscape :: k GroupName, GroupSpecifiersThatMatch, and \k in a class, which no production gives [NamedCaptureGroups] (B.1.2's ClassEscape reads ~NamedCaptureGroups, so SourceCharacterIdentityEscape admits k; the main grammar's IdentityEscape refuses it); /: accepted; the outcome holds with B.1.2 alone (without Annex B: error: SyntaxError); Annex B is required of a web-browser host and optional for any other; both engines answer B.1.2's patterns (Broiler.VM's JSD-0081, requirement R5, Annex B syntax where the specification allows it, and bundle sjl-11-001's correspondence, rows S4 and S7, same in both), so each engine's expectation is B.1.2's outcome
since: 31

